      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">*Bandwidth: unlimited in Amsterdam (Digital Realty AMS5) and Salt Lake City (SLC1); capped at 1 TB/month in Hong Kong. All plans include 1 IPv4 + /64 IPv6 + DDoS protection as standard. Source: cloudblast.io/pricing (August 2026).</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Hardware & Software Stack</h2>
      <p>The platform is consistent across every plan — there is no "good-better-best" hardware spectrum, only a capacity one. Cloudblast publishes the host node specs publicly (verified in the LowEndBox feature article's "Host Node Specs" block).</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">CPU & Memory</h3>
      <p>Every Cloudblast instance runs on AMD EPYC host hardware. The LowEndBox feature article confirms three node CPU types in production: <strong>EPYC 7551P</strong> (32-core Naples, 512 GB RAM per node), <strong>EPYC 7C13</strong> (Milan, 700 GB RAM per node), and <strong>EPYC 7663</strong> (Milan, 380 GB RAM per node). Whether a given VM lands on a 7551P, 7C13, or 7663 node depends on pool availability — Cloudblast does not currently let users pick the underlying CPU.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Storage</h3>
      <p>All plans ship with <strong>NVMe SSD</strong> storage. Capacity tops out at 200 GB on the highest VMA81 tier (64 GB RAM) — for heavy-disk workloads, the block storage add-on at €0.15/GB/mo is the practical answer. The 32 GB / 64 GB tiers (VMA71 / VMA81) jump storage to 140 GB / 200 GB, suggesting that Cloudblast sizes storage roughly proportional to RAM needs rather than as a separate dimension.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Virtualization</h3>
      <p>Cloudblast runs <strong>KVM/Proxmox</strong> virtualization. The LowEndBox article specifies this explicitly: every VMA plan is KVM-Proxmox, with full root access to the guest OS. Custom ISO uploads are not advertised (Cloudblast ships a Linux distribution selector and one-click app templates rather than arbitrary ISO upload).</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Network</h3>
      <p>Every instance ships a <strong>10 Gbps network port</strong> — that is the headline feature. DDoS protection is bundled as standard, with no separate subscription fee (a meaningful differentiator vs hosts that treat DDoS as a paid add-on at $5-15/mo). The IPv4 stack is a single address per instance with /64 IPv6; additional IPv4 addresses are €2.5/mo each.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Data Center Footprint (3 Active + 1 Coming)</h2>
      <p>Cloudblast's datacenter footprint is small but covers three strategically useful regions, verified from the live <a href="https://cloudblast.io/" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">cloudblast.io homepage</a> and the LowEndBox feature article:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-300">
        <li><strong>Amsterdam, Netherlands</strong> — Digital Realty AMS5, Test IPv4 <code>89.34.230.32</code>, looking glass <code>nl.lg.cloudblast.io</code></li>
        <li><strong>Salt Lake City, Utah, United States</strong> — SLC1, Test IPv4 <code>192.166.82.235</code>, looking glass <code>us.lg.cloudblast.io</code></li>
        <li><strong>Hong Kong SAR, China (APAC)</strong> — Eons Data, Test IPv4 <code>178.83.121.94</code>, looking glass <code>hk.lg.cloudblast.io</code></li>
        <li><strong>Birmingham, United Kingdom</strong> — listed as "coming soon" on cloudblast.io (not yet bookable)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The Amsterdam and Salt Lake City tiers ship <strong>unlimited bandwidth</strong>; the Hong Kong tier is <strong>capped at 1 TB/month</strong>. Buyers picking Hong Kong specifically for low-latency APAC access should size accordingly or run lighter workloads.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Who Cloudblast Is Best For</h2>
      <p>Three profiles fit Cloudblast well: budget DDoS-conscious buyers, hourly-billing workload runners, and one-click-app users.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">1. Budget Buyers Who Need DDoS Protection</h3>
      <p>Most €3-5/mo VPS plans either charge €5-15/mo extra for DDoS protection or skip it entirely. Cloudblast's bundled DDoS at this price point is unusual — if you are running a public-facing service (game server, VPN endpoint, web app with adversarial traffic) at the budget tier, Cloudblast covers a risk most peers do not.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">2. Hourly-Billing Workload Runners</h3>
      <p>The hourly billing model — €0.0049/hr at the entry tier — makes Cloudblast a strong fit for short-lived workloads: CI runners, scrapers, ephemeral dev sandboxes, batch jobs that run for a few hours per day. The panel lets you resize, rebuild, or delete from the web UI at any time.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">3. One-Click App Users (Coolify, n8n, Dokploy, Supabase + 12 more)</h3>
      <p>Cloudblast ships a one-click app catalog that includes <strong>Coolify, CyberPanel, Dokploy, FastPanel, n8n, Nextcloud, Nginx, OpenClaw, Plausible, Pritunl, Pterodactyl, Supabase, Traefik, WordPress, and Hermes Agent</strong>. If your stack fits one of those one-click templates, deployment is a single panel click rather than a manual install.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Where Cloudblast Falls Short</h2>
      <p>The same specialization that makes Cloudblast good for DDoS-conscious budget buyers becomes a ceiling for power users.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Only 3 Active Data Centers</h3>
      <p>Unlike <a href="/vps/vultr" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Vultr</a> (32+ locations), <a href="/vps/linode" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Linode (Akamai)</a> (11+), or <a href="/vps/hosthatch" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">HostHatch</a> (14 documented regions), Cloudblast runs three active regions. If your audience needs low-latency access from São Paulo, Mumbai, Sydney, or Tokyo (other than Hong Kong's Eons Data facility), Cloudblast is not the right pick.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Hong Kong 1 TB Bandwidth Cap</h3>
      <p>The Hong Kong location is capped at 1 TB/month while Amsterdam and Salt Lake City are unlimited. Buyers specifically choosing Hong Kong for low-latency APAC access need to size workloads to that cap, or pick Salt Lake City and accept higher trans-Pacific latency.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">No GPU Instances, No Managed Tier</h3>
      <p>Cloudblast is bare self-managed KVM. There is no GPU compute tier, no managed-Linux offering, no Plesk/cPanel option. For managed Linux with bundled security + cPanel, see <a href="/vps/tmdhosting" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">TMDHosting</a>. For GPU compute, see <a href="/vps/cherryservers" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Cherry Servers</a> or <a href="/vps/linode" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Linode (Akamai)</a>.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">New Vendor Track Record</h3>
      <p>Cloudblast's first major editorial coverage is from August 2026. There is no public uptime history, no long-term user base outside the LowEndTalk community, and no third-party SLA documentation. Buyers who need a 99.99% uptime guarantee with credit SLA should look at longer-tenured providers; Cloudblast's pricing page does not currently advertise one.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Comparison: Cloudblast vs Other Budget VPS Providers on VPSTier</h2>
      <p>We've reviewed 60+ VPS providers on vpstier.com. Here's how Cloudblast stacks up to the closest comparables.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Cloudblast vs RackNerd</h3>
      <p><a href="/vps/racknerd" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a> starts at $1.99/mo for 1 vCPU / 1 GB RAM / 25 GB SSD. Cloudblast's €3.6/mo entry is roughly 1.8× RackNerd's entry in absolute price, but Cloudblast bundles DDoS protection and a 10 Gbps port — both absent at the RackNerd entry tier. RackNerd remains the pure-budget Linux leader; Cloudblast wins on DDoS inclusion + 10 Gbps.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Cloudblast vs Vultr</h3>
      <p><a href="/vps/vultr" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Vultr</a> starts at $5/mo for 1 vCPU / 1 GB RAM / 25 GB SSD with hourly billing. Cloudblast VMA11 (€3.6/mo for 3 GB RAM, 20 GB NVMe) undercuts Vultr's entry on price-per-GB and includes DDoS standard, but Vultr ships 32+ global data centers vs Cloudblast's three. Vultr is the right pick for global coverage; Cloudblast for DDoS + 10 Gbps at the budget tier.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Cloudblast vs HostHatch</h3>
      <p><a href="/vps/hosthatch" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">HostHatch</a> starts at $4/mo for high-RAM EPYC across 14 documented regions. HostHatch wins on coverage and storage-per-dollar; Cloudblast wins on DDoS inclusion and the 10 Gbps port. If you need Asia-Pacific latency in a region Cloudblast does not cover, HostHatch is the better pick.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Cloudblast vs TMDHosting</h3>
      <p><a href="/vps/tmdhosting" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">TMDHosting</a> starts at $69/mo for fully managed cPanel VPS. Cloudblast and TMDHosting target different buyers entirely: TMDHosting is the "set-it-and-forget-it" managed service with bundled security + 24/7 human support; Cloudblast is bare self-managed KVM. Pick TMDHosting if you want cPanel and management; pick Cloudblast if you want hourly-billed self-managed at €3.6/mo with DDoS standard.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">FAQ: Cloudblast VPS in 2026</h2>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">What is the cheapest Cloudblast VPS plan?</h3>
      <p>The cheapest Cloudblast VPS plan is <strong>VMA11 at €3.6/month</strong> (or €0.0049/hour billed hourly). It includes 1 vCPU (AMD EPYC), 3 GB RAM, 20 GB NVMe SSD, a 10 Gbps port, 1 IPv4 + /64 IPv6, and DDoS protection as standard. Bandwidth is unlimited in Amsterdam and Salt Lake City; capped at 1 TB/month in Hong Kong.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Where are Cloudblast's data centers?</h3>
      <p>Cloudblast runs three active data centers: <strong>Amsterdam</strong> (Digital Realty AMS5, Netherlands), <strong>Salt Lake City</strong> (SLC1, Utah, United States), and <strong>Hong Kong</strong> (Eons Data, APAC). A Birmingham (UK) location is listed as "coming soon" on cloudblast.io as of August 2026.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Does Cloudblast include DDoS protection in the price?</h3>
      <p>Yes. <strong>DDoS protection is bundled as standard</strong> on every Cloudblast VPS plan at no additional cost. Combined with the 10 Gbps network port, it is one of the more DDoS-friendly entry-level offerings among 2026 budget hosts — most peers at this price either skip DDoS entirely or charge €5-15/mo extra for it.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">What virtualization and CPU hardware does Cloudblast use?</h3>
      <p>Cloudblast runs <strong>KVM/Proxmox virtualization</strong> on AMD EPYC host nodes. Verified node CPUs include <strong>EPYC 7551P</strong> (32-core, 512 GB RAM), <strong>EPYC 7C13</strong> (Milan, 700 GB RAM), and <strong>EPYC 7663</strong> (Milan, 380 GB RAM). Instances are billed by the hour and can be resized, rebuilt, or deleted from the self-service panel without commitment.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">How does Cloudblast compare to RackNerd and Vultr?</h3>
      <p>Cloudblast's €3.6/mo entry is competitive with RackNerd's $1.99 entry but includes DDoS protection and a 10 Gbps port that RackNerd does not. Vultr's $5/mo 1 GB tier is comparable to Cloudblast VMA11 (€3.6/mo) but Vultr ships 32+ global data centers vs Cloudblast's three. Cloudblast fits buyers who specifically want DDoS-included + 10 Gbps in one of three locations; RackNerd remains the pure-budget Linux leader; Vultr remains the global-coverage choice.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Final Verdict</h2>
      <p>Cloudblast's 2026 opening offer is one of the sharper entry-level deals we've seen in the budget VPS bracket — <strong>€3.6/mo for 1 vCPU / 3 GB RAM / 20 GB NVMe with 10 Gbps + DDoS standard</strong> across three useful regions (Amsterdam, Salt Lake City, Hong Kong). It is not the cheapest (RackNerd at $1.99 entry undercuts it), not the broadest (Vultr at 32+ locations wins coverage), and not the most established (Cloudblast's first major editorial coverage is from August 2026). What it is, is the right pick for buyers who specifically want DDoS-included + 10 Gbps at the budget tier without paying for a managed service.</p>

      <p>If you want DDoS-included budget VPS in one of three locations and can live with the small footprint, Cloudblast is a defensible choice. For pure-budget Linux VPS, see our <a href="/vps/racknerd" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd review</a>. For global coverage at €5/mo, see <a href="/vps/vultr" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Vultr</a>. For managed cPanel VPS at $69/mo, see <a href="/vps/tmdhosting" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">TMDHosting</a>.</p>

